"""Alternative prose for the entries whose text, written for the original encoding, states how that encoding lays the instruction out: the field
named here replaces the one in the entry (tools/mkmanual.py; the other fields of those entries are trimmed by memberprose.py).  The layouts themselves are the
charts, generated from the grammars; what is written here is meaning only."""

ATOMNOTE = ('The sub-opcodes and the semantics of this group are specified. The forms below name the roles; the placement of the operands (three registers with the address in a register, four for `CAS` and `CASDB`, in the order written) is open ([J.9.3](appendix-j-open.md#j93)).')

NOTES = {
    'MOV': 'A register is always written in full: a narrow load sign-extends to 64 bits and there is no partial-register write anywhere. The register-to-register forms of the sized moves are the conversion set: `MOVB R1, R2` sign-extends the low byte of `R1` into `R2`.',
    'MOVZ': 'There is no zero-extending 64-bit move (it would be `MOVQ`). The register-to-register forms are the zero-extending half of the conversion set.',
    'LEA': '`LEA 0(PC), Rd` is how the program counter is read: it puts the address of the `LEA` itself in `Rd`. The base of every PC-relative displacement is the address of the first parcel of the instruction.',
    'MOVU': 'The ordinary moves fault on a misaligned address (the alignment fault is the tag check of section 9.5); these are the explicit forms for data that is not aligned.',
    'ADDM': 'The forms with a memory destination are in the ordinary memory families of the encoding, with every addressing form (errata 87).',
    'BCC': 'Condition numbers: `al` 0 (`BR`), `ne` 1, `eq` 2, `pl` 3, `mi` 4, `vc` 5, `vs` 6, `cc`/`hs` 7, `cs`/`lo` 8, `ge` 9, `lt` 10, `gt` 11, `le` 12, `hi` 13, `los` 14. Conditions on the flags: `ne` `!Z`; `eq` `Z`; `pl` `!N`; `mi` `N`; `vc` `!V`; `vs` `V`; `cc` `!C`; `cs` `C`; `ge` `N == V`; `lt` `N != V`; `gt` `!Z and N == V`; `le` `Z or N != V`; `hi` `!C and !Z`; `los` `C or Z`. The displacement counts 2-byte parcels; the reach of each form is in its chart.',
    'CBCC': 'The family has several forms for each condition (the layouts of this entry): the second operand is a register or an immediate of some width, and the reach differs. A `cbCC` that no form reaches is assembled by `as64` as `CMP` and `bCC`, which **writes the flags** (errata 87, assumption 6; the integration measured 17 such sites in 34,524, errata 90).',
    'BTAG': 'For runtimes that keep a 3-bit type tag in the low bits of a pointer (`lowtag`). The displacement is signed and counts parcels.',
    'SET': 'The condition is numbered as the low nibble of the branch opcodes (`al` 0, `ne` 1, `eq` 2, ... `los` 14).',
    'RET': 'Not privileged: it is the partner of `CALL`.',
    'FRINT': 'The rounding mode is in the instruction, never in a mode bit. The alternative spellings `FRINTD`, `FRINTDZ`, ... are aliases.',
    'SHIFT': 'The count is read as an unsigned 64-bit value, so a count of 64 or more shifts the value entirely away. Small immediate counts have one-parcel forms (the layouts of this entry); `ASL` and `ASR` are the by-one forms; rotates are `ROR` and `RORI`.',
    'ASL': 'The by-one form of `SHL`.', 'ASR': 'The by-one form of `SAR`.',
    'PUSHM': 'The registers are pushed in ascending order, so `POPM` with the same mask restores them. The mask is the low 32 bits of the number (errata 87, assumption 7); the one-parcel form takes an 8-bit mask scaled by 256, which names `R8` to `R15`.',
    'POPM': 'The mirror of `PUSHM`.',
    'PUSH': '',
    'TRAP': 'The replacement for the PDP-11 `IOT` and `EMT`. In these encodings `TRAP` and `TRAPL` are one word with an 8-bit vector number (`docs/encoding-proposal.md`).',
    'PUSHX': 'For a handler that wants to be interrupted: it can save the exception state and lower the priority.',
    'MFSR': 'The register is chosen by name. A write to `PTBR`, `ASID` or `MMUCTL` flushes the translation buffer.\n\n{{SYSREGS}}',
    'COUNT': '`CTZ` is the VAX `FFS`. Bit manipulation is core on a machine that does filesystem and bignum work, not an extension.',
    'RORI': '', 'DSHR': 'x86 `SHRD`, ARM `EXTR`. A 128-bit left shift by k is `DSHR hi, hi, lo, #(64-k)` then `SHL #k, lo`.',
    'EXTB': 'Position and width are immediate operands; a width of 64 cannot be encoded (errata 87, assumption 8).',
    'JMPI': 'The one deferred reference the architecture admits, for control transfer only: the PLT stub (`JMPI got_slot(PC)`) and virtual-method dispatch (`CALLI vtbl_off(Robj)`). It needs a memory operand.',
    'FMADD': 'The alternative spellings `FMADDD`, ... are aliases.',
    'FDIV': '`FDIV` and `FSQRT` are multi-cycle sequencer operations and rarely appear in a one-parcel form. The alternative spellings `FDIVD`, `FSQRTD` are aliases.',
}
for _i in ('CAS', 'SWAP', 'XADD', 'XAND', 'XMAX'):
    NOTES[_i] = ATOMNOTE
NOTES['LLSC'] = ('A short `LL`/`SC` sequence must eventually succeed if no other initiator keeps writing the granule (the RISC-V guarantee for constrained sequences). ' + ATOMNOTE
                 + ' The result convention of `SC` is part of the open placement ([J.9.3](appendix-j-open.md#j93)).')
EXC = {'LEA': 'The operand must name memory (a register or an immediate is not a form). No memory access is made, so the instruction takes no memory fault. A reserved encoding aborts with the illegal-instruction vector.'}

# The "Why it is here" paragraphs rewritten for the prefix-coded encoding where the original paragraph mixes a rationale with a statement about the original encoding;
# the rationale, the precedent and the measurements are kept (a measurement of the original encoding is said to be one).
STORY = {
    'MOVU': 'Data alignment is architectural on this machine (9.2.11): an ordinary quad access to an address that is not a multiple of 8 aborts, and that abort doubles as the tag check for tagged pointers; a load form that ignores low pointer bits is rejected for that reason. Packed on-disk and network structures still need unaligned access, so the architecture has explicit unaligned forms rather than weakening the rule for every load. No further reason is recorded for these four.',
    'ADDM': 'These are the read-modify-write forms with a memory destination. They cover the case where the second operand is a register, for example `BISM Rmask, flags(DP)`. The compiled corpus has 265 sites (1.6 KB) for them, measured for the original encoding, which is why they are not worth an opcode of their own there.',
    'SOB': 'Taken from the PDP-11. The PDP-11\'s could only branch backward (a 6-bit unsigned displacement); this one branches either way. Against the `cbnz` idiom of other architectures it does not save bytes (`DEC` plus `BNE` is also four in the original encoding) but it saves an instruction issue, a cycle per iteration on a machine that does not pipeline: same size, one instruction fewer (static `dec; bne` pairs exist).',
    'CBCC': 'A fused compare-and-branch, estimated with `RORI32` at 22 KB (1.3%) of the image of the original encoding. There the wide-immediate form removes 11,735 instructions (3.4%) and 1,828 bytes, and the register-register compare-and-branch removes 4,631 instructions (1.4%) and 142 bytes. It is not a replacement for flags: LoongArch and RISC-V chose fused compare-and-branch instead of flags, and this architecture declines that (9.20) because it needs a comparator in the branch unit and a third read port; here the fused form is added beside the flags. `CBCC` writes no flags.',
    'BTAG': 'The tag branches `BTAG` and `BNTAG` and the table dispatch `JTAG` make tagged pointers cheap to use rather than merely possible. Because quad-aligned objects have zero in the low three bits, the tag is free, and an unaligned access is a fault. The compiler for C never emits them (the corpus shows none); they are for Lisp and other tagged runtimes.',
    'PUSHM': 'The VAX procedure entry mask was the useful half of `CALLS`; `PUSHM` and `POPM` keep that half without the frame (hardware call frames ran slower than the sequence on later VAXes). The one-parcel forms take an 8-bit mask for `R8` to `R15`, because all 2,392 push sites and 2,392 pop sites in the measured corpus use a mask within `R8` to `R15`.',
    'JMPI': 'Exactly one deferred reference, for control transfer only: the PLT stub of every dynamically linked program (`JMPI got_slot(PC)`) and virtual-method dispatch (`CALLI vtbl_off(Robj)`), each two instructions and a scratch register without it. It is admitted because it breaks none of the reasons deferred modes are removed (no register is side-effected, the reference is in the branch path and not the operand path, a fault is clean by ordering). Jump tables through `JMPI` are not smaller than a compare chain, because a table entry is 8 bytes and a case in a compare chain is also 8.',
    'COUNT': 'Bit manipulation is not an extension on a machine that does filesystem work, bignum arithmetic and the like. The counts are the VAX `FFS` and `FFC` idiom (find first set, find first clear), and LoongArch, which has `CLO`, `CLZ`, `CTO` and `CTZ` in its base, is the model for putting them there. The compiler emits none of them, since C has no operator for them; the corpus shows zero uses.',
    'POPCNT': 'One of the counts; it is also the building block of `XNORPOP`. A compiler needs `__builtin_popcount` to emit it, so the corpus shows no use (see `COUNT`).',
    'BITREV': 'Borrowed with the other LoongArch bit operations (`REVB`, `REVH`, `BITREV`). No workload from the corpus is recorded.',
    'ROR': 'One rotate direction suffices and `ROR` is the one to keep: `ROL x, #n` is exactly `ROR x, #(64-n)`, and a funnel shifter yields `ROR` directly. No rotate is through carry (see `DSHR`). `ROR` is a bit operation and not an ALU operation: cryptographic code rotates by compile-time constants, so an immediate form is needed either way, and the register-count form has almost no users. Rotates write no flags.',
    'EXTB': 'Bit-field extract and insert are the VAX `EXTV`, `EXTZV` and `INSV` idiom. The compiler does not use them: the shift-pair extraction pattern occurs 151 times in 26 files of the corpus, about 600 bytes at best (0.03%), and there are four true bit-field extracts in all. They are for hand-written protocol and bit-stream code.',
    'XNORPOP': 'It serves binary-activation networks, Hamming distance, bitset intersection counts and counting a collector\'s mark bitmap. It does not serve sub-1-bit language models: their activations (in the LittleBit work) are FP16 and the inner loop is a conditional add, not a popcount. The sub-1-bit kernel is bandwidth-bound and belongs to a bus-mastering accelerator, not an opcode (chapter 8).',
    'MSET': 'With a zero value it is the range-zeroing instruction, so `bzero` and the clearing of `calloc` need no separate opcode.',
    'MSCN': '`MSCN` is `LOCC` and `memchr`; `MSCNN` fills the `SKPC` gap (the first byte that is *not* equal). Both cost almost no hardware. With an explicit count `MSCN` gives `strnlen`, and with `MCPY` a bounded `strcpy` in two instructions. NUL-terminated string instructions (the System/z `MVST`, `SRST`, `CLST`) are rejected, because they encode an unbounded read, the largest single source of memory-safety failures in C (9.17).',
    'MVAL': 'The UTF-8 validity comparators already built for `UTF8DEC`, wired to the streaming sequencer already built for `MCPY`, so it costs almost no hardware. The alternative, a SWAR loop (SIMD within a register), runs at about two to three cycles a byte and is respectable, which is why the justification is cost and not throughput.',
    'MULHU': 'The unsigned partner of `MULH`, for the same reason (the high half of the product). `MULHSU`, signed times unsigned for a bignum\'s top limb, is reserved because it can be built from `MULH` with one correction.',
    'SET': 'The first instruction written with a condition word: one instruction, `SET cc, Rd`, rather than one opcode per condition, with the condition numbered like the low nibble of the branch opcodes. It turns a comparison used as a value into 0 or 1 without a branch. gcc `-Os` for amd64 emits 673 `set<cc>` on the corpus, and `SET` would remove about six bytes per site, around 0.4% of code; the compiler\'s first pass turns such a comparison into branches before its second pass sees it, so the saving is not reached in compiled code.',
    'FSINGLE': '`binary64` has first-class opcodes because on a 64-bit machine it is the common case; `binary32` has the same operations, held in the low 32 bits of a register and sign-extended like every other 32-bit value (see also the ABI). The naming rule is an `F` in front of the integer mnemonic (`FADD`) and a size suffix for the other formats (`FADD16`); the older spellings (`FADDS` and so on) are aliases.',
    'FMADD': 'The whole fused multiply-add family is in the instruction set because its absence would be the single worst gap in the floating-point set: one rounding instead of two, and the inner step of dot products, polynomial evaluation and every matrix kernel. It has four register operands. It replaces the VAX `POLY`, which was the best answer before fused multiply-add.',
    'SWAP': 'One of the read-modify-write atomics (see `CAS`). No separate reason is recorded for it.',
    'XADD': 'One of the fetch-and-operate atomics (see `CAS`), in the place of the PDP-11\'s read-modify-write instructions, which were indivisible on the Unibus through the `DATIP`/`DATO` pair (7.1).',
    'XAND': 'The fetch-and-and, or and xor atomics (see `CAS`). No separate reason is recorded for them.',
    'TRAP': 'In the original encoding `TRAPL` existed so that the vector range of 256 and the two-byte common case could both be kept (`TRAP #n` for `n` below 16 is two bytes, and the long form reaches 256); here one word carries an 8-bit vector number. `IOT` and `EMT`, which were `TRAP` at two particular vectors on the PDP-11, are not provided.',
    'MTDP': '`DP` descends from the 6502\'s zero page and is kept for code density (a short displacement from `DP`); it is also the thread pointer in user mode and, in kernel mode, the per-CPU pointer that an SMP kernel wants (7.4). `MTDP` and `MFDP` read and write the current mode\'s copy.',
}
